statement:
Given the `root` of a binary tree, return the values of its **boundary**: the nodes met when walking around the tree's outline counter-clockwise, defined exactly as the concatenation of

1. the root;
2. the **left boundary**, from top to bottom;
3. all **leaves**, from left to right;
4. the **right boundary**, from bottom to top.

A leaf is a node with no children, but the root is never treated as a leaf (a one-node tree's boundary is just the root).

The left boundary begins at the root's left child and is empty if the root has none. From a node on it, the next node is its left child if it has one, and otherwise its right child. The path stops before it reaches a leaf: that last leaf is reported with the leaves, not with the left boundary. The right boundary is the mirror image: it begins at the root's right child (empty if there is none), prefers the right child over the left one, and also excludes the leaf it ends at. No node is listed twice.

validator:
function validate([root]) {
  if (!Array.isArray(root) || root.length === 0 || root[0] === null) return 'The tree needs at least one node.';
  let nodes = 1;
  let slots = 2;
  for (let i = 1; i < root.length; i++) {
    if (slots === 0) return 'Some values have no parent in the level-order list.';
    slots--;
    if (root[i] !== null) {
      nodes++;
      slots += 2;
    }
  }
  if (nodes > 10000) return 'The tree has at most 10^4 nodes.';
  if (root.some(v => v !== null && (!Number.isInteger(v) || v < -1000 || v > 1000))) return 'Each value is an integer between -1000 and 1000.';
  return null;
}

